F697|THE FIRST ZERO-COST FALSIFICATION TEST PASSES — THE VENDOR DOCS ARE SERVED, 4789 BYTES EXACTLY, AND NOTHING IN THE ENGINE EVER READS THEM|✅ THE CHANGE LIST PRESCRIBES FOUR TESTS THAT COST NOTHING AND RUN ON TODAY'S BINARY, EACH ABLE TO KILL ITS CHANGE BEFORE THE ~4-FLEET-DAY REBUILD IS SPENT. **THE FIRST ONE PASSES.** `/v1/docs` on the live run's vendor mock returns **EXACTLY 4789 BYTES**, the figure the C10 proposal predicted. So the doc_fetch arm is NOT dead on arrival - there is real document content sitting there to fetch. **Thirty seconds of curl instead of a hundred minutes of fleet time**, and the port was read from the live run's own log rather than assumed, because the mock binds a randomised port per run (8930..9002) and a fixed guess would have produced a false 'connection refused' that reads exactly like a dead endpoint. 🔴 **AND THE COMBINATION WITH THE RESEARCH DIAGNOSIS IS DAMNING.** The vendor docs ARE being served, in full, on every run. Meanwhile `research_tools` defaults FALSE (swarm.rs:1289) so `has_lookup` is false and **EVERY SCOUT IS TOLD 'You cannot look anything up on this run, so answer from what you already know'**; `doc_fetch` also defaults false so the orchestrator's own deterministic fetch at :24140-24205 never runs, and **ZERO `doc_fetched` events exist in the entire corpus**. **THE DOCUMENT IS ON THE WIRE AND NOTHING IN THE ENGINE OPENS IT.** 🎯 WHICH EXPLAINS THE STRANGEST NUMBER OF THE DAY. Research minutes correlate at **r = MINUS 0.650 WITHIN BUILD (t = -2.90)** with **TIER C - the VENDOR CONTRACT tier, the one the scorer says is 'only reachable by reading the vendor docs'**. Scouts spend seven minutes reasoning from memory about a document they are forbidden to open, and the longer they spend the worse that tier scores. **THAT IS NOT A TUNING PROBLEM, IT IS A DISCONNECTED WIRE.** 📋 REMAINING ZERO-COST TESTS, WITH THEIR OWN KILL CONDITIONS: **C13** - 13 cells still hold built trees (the list expected 12), replay must reproduce 