F698|THE RED-TEAM CAUGHT A LIVE BUG I INTRODUCED THIS MORNING — MY OWN VOID FIX WAS SET TO DESTROY REAL RUNS, AND THE CONTROL THAT CLEARED IT WAS TRUE AND USELESS|🔴 THIS MORNING I ADDED `harness_ok is False` TO THE VOID PREDICATE AND VERIFIED IT SAFE: **'of 118 rows, ZERO would be voided by the harness clause alone, and ZERO surviving non-void rows carry harness_ok False'.** That check was TRUE. **IT WAS ALSO USELESS, because the live corpus is a SURVIVORSHIP SNAPSHOT (F694/F695) - I validated a rule against the very population it had already been emptied of.** 📊 RE-DERIVED MYSELF ACROSS ALL **31 RUN TREES ON DISK**, not the snapshot. The boolean covers **TWO COMPLETELY DIFFERENT FAILURES**: **654 rows fail st-2** with the detail 'invariant pass CRASHED: no run log' - the fleet-outage phantoms, 0.1 seconds, no pool, nothing ran, correctly void. **THREE rows fail st-1** (dispatch/completion pairing) and are **REAL 112-to-124-MINUTE RUNS WITH POOL 3 OF 3, SCORING 0.7186, 0.672 AND 0.819 - ONE OF THEM `retarget_off-n3-r0`, A REAL LEVER RUN**, which is the scarcest data this campaign has. **654 AGAINST 3 IS NOT A CLOSE CALL, AND I HAD LUMPED THEM TOGETHER.** 🛠️ FIXED: the void now keys on the **st-2 SIGNATURE** ('no run log'), never on the boolean. A self-test that failed for any other reason still MARKS the unit suspect via `harness_ok` - which is what that field is for - but can no longer silently erase a run that actually executed. The regression test now asserts BOTH directions explicitly: st-2 voids, **st-1 does NOT**. ⚠️ AND THE HONEST SCOPE OF THE REPAIR: **ZERO ROWS WERE RESTORED IN THE LIVE TREE, because all three st-1 runs sit in PARKED trees and were never in the live corpus to begin with.** So this fix PREVENTS FUTURE DESTRUCTION rather than recovering past data - and that is precisely the value, because the sweep restarts with this code and would otherwise have voided the next real st-1 run it produced. 💡 THE LESSON IS THE SHARPEST OF THE DAY AND IT IS ABOUT MY OWN VERIFICATION HABIT, NOT ABOUT THIS BUG: **A CONTROL RUN AGAINST A CORPUS THAT HAS ALREADY LOST THE CASES IT WOULD CATCH WILL ALWAYS PASS.** I have said 'prove the instrument can see the thing at all' a dozen times today and applied it to the READER while never asking whether the POPULATION still contained the thing. Checking a filter against data the filter's target has already been removed from is not a control - it is a mirror
F699|THE PHANTOMS WERE STILL LIVE IN A THIRD CORPUS ALL DAY — tiers.jsonl HAD 125 OF 130 ROWS MARKED VALID, AND THE BACKFILL CONTROL CAUGHT FIVE MORE THE JOIN COULD NOT REACH|🔴 THE RED-TEAM'S LAST LIVE ITEM: **`tiers.jsonl` WAS NEVER BACKFILLED.** While I spent the morning voiding 104 phantoms in the result files and reporting the corpus as clean, **A THIRD CORPUS STILL CARRIED 125 OF 130 ROWS MARKED void:false** - including every phantom. Any analysis reading it would have picked up the full contamination, and `tiers.jsonl` is the RICHEST of the three: it carries tiers AND engine_build AND the void flag, which neither loop.log (no tiers, no build) nor the result files (survivorship snapshot) manage together. 🛠️ BACKFILLED FROM THE CORRECTED RESULT FILES on (cell, finished_at): **121 of 130 matched, 104 FLIPPED to void.** The pre-image is preserved at `tiers.jsonl.pre-F699` - I copied before writing, because overwriting the only record of a contamination is how the evidence of a bug disappears with the bug. ✅ **AND THE CONTROL EARNED ITS KEEP IMMEDIATELY.** I asserted that no surviving row may have a sub-5-minute wall. **IT FAILED.** Five rows survived at **0.00 minutes, score 0.0, all finished within FOUR SECONDS of each other at 2026-08-05T08:04:37-41** - the Aug-5 fleet-outage burst. They were unreachable by the join because **their result files had since been OVERWRITTEN by later runs of the same cell (F695)**, so there was no row to inherit a void flag from. **THE SURVIVORSHIP BUG WAS BLOCKING ITS OWN CLEANUP.** 🛠️ Voided those five by SIGNATURE instead of by join - a run of under 60 seconds that scored exactly 0.0 never executed - and the control now passes in both directions: **16 surviving rows, minimum wall 66.3 minutes, mean 96.3, scores spanning 0.3587 to 0.9760, split 8 one-node and 8 three-node.** No phantom-length row survives; the survivors look like real builds. 💡 THE PATTERN THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN OBVIOUS HOURS AGO: **THERE ARE THREE CORPORA AND I HAVE BEEN FIXING THEM ONE AT A TIME AS EACH IS DISCOVERED.** Result files (survivorship snapshot, now voided), loop.log (complete for score/wall, no tiers or build), tiers.jsonl (richest, contaminated until now). **A FIX APPLIED TO ONE CORPUS IS NOT A FIX** - and I reported the corpus as clean three separate times today while two of the three still carried the contamination
